tio'] = np.log1p(df['branch_to_operator_ratio'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# 2nd Added Feature: operator_operand_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of unique operators (`uniq_Op`) to unique operands (`uniq_Opnd`). This measure reflects the sophistication and complexity of program logic in terms of the interplay between operators and operands.\n# Usefulness: Complexity often arises from the way operators and operands interact in the code. Examining their proportional relationship sheds light on whether a module uses an efficient or overly complicated logic structure. This may correlate with defect likelihood, as excessive operand/operator imbalance can introduce bugs.\n\ndf['operator_operand_ratio'] = df['uniq_Op'] / (df['uniq_Opnd'] + 1e-8)  # Adding a small constant to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n```python\n# 3rd Added Feature: rolling_mean_halstead_difficulty\n# Feature description: Rolling mean of `halstead_difficulty_per_loc` computed over the preceding 3 samples. This adds temporal (sequence-based) context to the feature engineering process, using the trends across modules to better understand code difficulty patterns.\n# Usefulness: Over time, code difficulty is likely to evolve in sequential codebases (e.g., adjacent modules), reflecting the progression of a software development cycle. Capturing this evolution matters for defect prediction because spikes or drops in difficulty could indicate rushed or refactored code sections prone to errors.\n\ndf['rolling_mean_halstead_difficulty'] = df['halstead_difficulty_per_loc'].rolling(3, min_periods=1).mean()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column norm_branch_to_operator is dropped\n# The column norm_branch_to_operator is essentially a normalized version of branch_to_operator_ratio using min-max scaling, but the extreme skewness (Mean = 0.0074, Std = 0.0604) makes this feature less reliable for predictive modeling. Transformation techniques (e.g., logarithmic scaling) are more suitable in reducing skewness while preserving relative differences among values. Additionally, `log_branch_to_halstead_ratio` provides a better representation using logarithm, making this column redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['norm_branch_to_operator'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column operator_operand_interaction is dropped\n# The column operator_operand_interaction duplicates a calculation already encapsulated by `branch_to_operator_ratio`. It offers limited utility as its range spans multiple orders of magnitude (0.0 to 145065.0) creating instability for modeling, while its distribution is overly concentrated in lower ranges (Median = 66.0). The newly added `operator_operand_ratio` focuses directly on the interplay between operands and operators with better scaling properties, making this feature redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['operator_operand_interaction'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column comment_density is dropped\n# The column comment_density is highly sparse, with a Median and Q3 of 0.0, indicating that most of the modules lack comments entirely. While comments are valuable for developers, their complete absence in the majority of cases means they contribute less to defect prediction (as defects are more related to code complexity rather than comments), and including this feature may add noise to the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['comment_density'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
