2026-05-24 01:03:34,151 - INFO - Selected best state: ("### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering:\n\n#### 1. Creating Ratios of Key Metrics to Normalize Complexity Across Modules\nSince loc (lines of code) varies significantly across modules, normalizing complexity metrics (like cyclomatic complexity, Halstead difficulty, etc.) by loc can reveal how dense or complex the code is relative to its size.\n\npython\n# cyclomatic_complexity_per_loc\n# Cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) per line of code, representing how dense the module's decision-making paths are relative to its size.\n# Usefulness: This helps to understand whether smaller modules are disproportionately complex, a potential defect indicator.\ndf['cyclomatic_complexity_per_loc'] = df['v(g)'] / df['loc']\n\n# halstead_difficulty_per_loc\n# Halstead difficulty (d) per line of code, quantifying how challenging it is to write and understand per unit of code.\n# Usefulness: A high value could indicate areas more prone to human error and defects.\ndf['halstead_difficulty_per_loc'] = df['d'] / df['loc']\n\n# halstead_intelligence_per_loc\n# Halstead intelligence (i) divided by loc, representing the intelligence quotient per line of code.\n# Usefulness: High values can indicate maintainable code, while low values could flag potential defect hotspots.\ndf['halstead_intelligence_per_loc'] = df['i'] / df['loc']\nend\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Quantifying Code Comment Density\nThe presence or absence of comments (lOComment) relative to the code (lOCode) or total module size (loc) can often indicate maintainability. Well-documented code is typically easier to debug, which may correlate to fewer defects.\n\npython\n# comment_density\n# Proportion of comment lines (lOComment) to total code lines (lOCode).\n# Usefulness: A higher comment_density can be an indicator of well-explained and understandable code, potentially reducing defects.\ndf['comment_density'] = df['lOComment'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-9)\n\n# blank_lines_per_loc\n# Proportion of blank lines (lOBlank) to total lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: Modules with more blank lines can reflect better formatting and modularization, which often coincide with fewer errors.\ndf['blank_lines_per_loc'] = df['lOBlank'] / df['loc']\n\n# code_and_comment_ratio\n# Ratio of code and comment lines (locCodeAndComment) to code size (loc).\n# Usefulness: Combines two metrics to give a single measure of total explanatory content relative to the coding effort.\ndf['code_and_comment_ratio'] = df['locCodeAndComment'] / df['loc']\nend\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Encoding Interaction Between Complexity, Operators, and Branching Logic\nComplex interplay between multiple features can yield valuable insights, such as how well-structured the logic is relative to operators or branches. Examining the ratio or product of branchCount with Halstead and McCabe metrics can expose such interactions.\n\npython\n# branch_difficulty_ratio\n# Ratio of branch count (branchCount) to Halstead difficulty (d).\n# Usefulness: Modules with a large number of branches and high difficulty can indicate convoluted logic prone to defects.\ndf['branch_difficulty_ratio'] = df['branchCount'] / (df['d'] + 1e-9)\n\n# branch_to_operator_ratio\n# Ratio of branch count (branchCount) to total operators (total_Op).\n# Usefulness: Explores whether the branching logic is proportional to the number of operations, highlighting potential control-flow issues.\ndf['branch_to_operator_ratio'] = df['branchCount'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-9)\n\n# operator_operand_interaction\n# Product of total operators (total_Op) and total operands (total_Opnd).\n# Usefulness: A high value indicates a denser operation-to-operand relationship, which might correlate with convoluted or error-prone code.\ndf['operator_operand_interaction'] = df['total_Op'] * df['total_Opnd']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Redundant or Less Informative Features\n\nCertain features may offer limited additional value once enhanced versions or ratios are created, such as simple counts of lines or blank/comment lines in isolation. Here's how to rationalize their removal:\n\npython\n# Dropping redundant columns after feature engineering\n# Explanation: lOCode, lOComment, lOBlank, and locCodeAndComment are replaced by ratios and derived metrics that better represent interaction and density.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOCode', 'lOComment', 'lOBlank', 'locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation: Raw counts like total_Op and total_Opnd are replaced by their interaction (operator_operand_interaction) and branch relationships.\ndf.drop(columns=['total_Op', 'total_Opnd'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Proposed Features\nThe proposed features focus on normalization (ratios), density (relationships), and interaction effects to better capture defect-prone characteristics of the software modules:\n1. Normalized simplicity/complexity: Ratios like cyclomatic_complexity_per_loc reveal insights specific to each module's size.\n2. Documentation quality indicators: Metrics like comment_density highlight the maintainability of code.\n3. Complex logical interactions: Combining branching logic with operators and difficulty adds depth to understanding structural deficiencies.\n\nThese additions should improve the downstream model's predictive performance by emphasizing interpretable and defect-relevant relationships within the dataset.", "Here are three feature engineering steps designed to potentially improve prediction performance by creating new features that capture additional signal from existing data. These transformations leverage the provided statistical operators and dataset characteristics.\n\n### Step 1: Interaction Feature - log_branch_operator_interaction\nThis feature captures the relationship between branchCount and operator_operand_interaction using a logarithmic transformation to reduce any skewness in their original distribution.\n\npython\n# log_branch_operator_interaction\n# Logarithmic transformation of operator-operant interaction multiplied by branch count\n# Usefulness: By using a logarithmic transformation, this feature can capture the non-linear relationship between branch complexity and operator-operand interactions, especially when the raw interaction term may have a skewed distribution.\ndf['log_branch_operator_interaction'] = np.log1p(df['branchCount'] * df['operator_operand_interaction'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Ratio Normalization Feature - norm_branch_to_operator\nThis feature normalizes the branch_to_operator_ratio using the min-max scaling approach to bring values into a 0-1 range, making the feature's contribution comparable to others during model training.\n\npython\n# norm_branch_to_operator\n# Normalized version of branch_to_operator_ratio using min-max scaling\n# Usefulness: The original branch_to_operator_ratio feature has extreme outliers, so normalizing it will ensure that its scale doesn't disproportionately affect the model, improving the model's ability to generalize.\ndf['norm_branch_to_operator'] = (df['branch_to_operator_ratio'] - df['branch_to_operator_ratio'].min()) / \\\n                                (df['branch_to_operator_ratio'].max() - df['branch_to_operator_ratio'].min())\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Composite Feature - cyclomatic_difficulty_product\nThis feature combines cyclomatic_complexity_per_loc and halstead_difficulty_per_loc into a single feature using a multiplicative interaction. This reflects complexity per line of code along two dimensions: decision-making and difficulty.\n\npython\n# cyclomatic_difficulty_product\n# Product of cyclomatic complexity per line of code and Halstead difficulty per line of code\n# Usefulness: Captures a combined measure of complexity and difficulty per line of code, which could be a strong indicator of defect-prone modules when both measures are high or interact non-linearly.\ndf['cyclomatic_difficulty_product'] = df['cyclomatic_complexity_per_loc'] * df['halstead_difficulty_per_loc']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Drop Irrelevant Columns (if necessary)\nSome columns, such as those that are likely highly correlated or redundant, can introduce noise into the model. Dropping these columns can lead to better model interpretability and performance. \n\n#### Example: Dropping branch_difficulty_ratio\npython\n# Explanation why the column branch_difficulty_ratio is dropped\n# The 'branch_difficulty_ratio' column is highly similar to the 'branch_to_operator_ratio' column, with overlapping definitions and range issues such as extremely large values. Retaining both could introduce multicollinearity issues, so we drop this column.\ndf.drop(columns=['branch_difficulty_ratio'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Added Columns:\n- log_branch_operator_interaction: Captures logarithmic interaction between branch count and operator-operand complexity to highlight relationships in a non-linear scale.\n- norm_branch_to_operator: Mitigates the effects of extreme outliers in branch-to-operator ratio by normalizing the values.\n- cyclomatic_difficulty_product: Combines cyclomatic complexity and Halstead difficulty in a multiplicative interaction to better account for modules with high interdependencies or complexity.\n\n### Why These Features Are Useful:\n1. Normalization of skewed features reduces the penalty of extreme outliers, which may otherwise bias the downstream model.\n2. Log and Multiplicative Transformations allow for non-linear patterns to emerge.\n3. Combining Complexity Indicators highlights modules with a combination of dense decision-making paths and operand/operator interaction, which are often defect-prone.\n\nThese features, along with dropping redundant attributes, can make the dataset more informative for the downstream task of defect prediction.", "python\n# Feature name: cyclomatic_density\n# Feature description: The ratio of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) per line of code to its Halstead difficulty per line of code. This feature highlights the balance between module complexity and the mathematical difficulties in the implementation.\n# Usefulness: This feature provides a derived measure of how efficiently a module handles complexity as a function of its logical and operational density. Higher values may indicate more fault-prone modules since inefficiency between code complexity and operational difficulty can introduce defects.\ndf['cyclomatic_density'] = df['halstead_difficulty_per_loc'] / (df['cyclomatic_difficulty_product'] + 1e-10)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: defect_comment_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of the comment_density to operator_operand_interaction. This explores whether the proportion of comments in the code sufficiently scales with operator-operand interaction (a proxy for code activity).\n# Usefulness: Well-commented code is generally less error-prone, especially in areas with higher complexity or interactivity (operator-operand-heavy). This feature could help highlight modules that lack documentation in critical code sections and may correlate with defects.\ndf['defect_comment_ratio'] = df['comment_density'] / (df['operator_operand_interaction'] + 1e-10)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature name: normalized_code_complexity\n# Feature description: Normalized version of the product of Halstead difficulty per line and unique operators (uniq_Op) across the dataset using Min-Max scaling.\n# Usefulness: While Halstead difficulty quantifies cognitive effort, combining it with the number of unique operators accounts for code diversity. Normalization helps emphasize the modules with relatively higher risk for defects due to high complexity.\ndf['normalized_code_complexity'] = (df['cyclomatic_difficulty_product'] * df['uniq_Op']) / (df['cyclomatic_difficulty_product'].max() * df['uniq_Op'].max())\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column norm_branch_to_operator is dropped\n# The column norm_branch_to_operator is redundant because its original raw feature, branch_to_operator_ratio, is already present. \n# Keeping both leads to multicollinearity, which could negatively impact the model's interpretability and performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['norm_branch_to_operator'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\n# The column l (Halstead program length) has a limited feature range ([0.0, 2.0]). It adds less variance to the dataset and might not significantly impact defect prediction when better-engineered features that incorporate l, such as cyclomatic_difficulty_product, are already available.\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column branchCount is dropped\n# The column branchCount is represented in multiple other derived features, such as operator_operand_interaction and branch_to_operator_ratio. This leads to its marginal contribution in providing unique information for defect prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['branchCount'], inplace=True)\nend"), with improvements -
